title: 'eth-rpc: add a CLI option to rate-limit the historical block sync'
doc:
- audience: [Node Dev, Node Operator]
  description: |-
    eth-rpc has a new option, `--historical-sync-max-blocks-per-sec <N>`, that limits the
    historical block sync to at most `N` blocks per second on average, to reduce the load on the
    node. It requires `--eth-pruning archive` (the default); with any other value, eth-rpc fails
    to start. Without the option there is no limit, so existing deployments need no change.

    After a restart, the historical sync now first syncs the blocks from the latest finalized
    block down to the range it has already synced, and then the blocks below that range.
    Previously, it synced both at the same time.

    The "Fresh sync", "Resuming sync" and "Tail exists without Head" log lines now also show the
    stored first EVM block.

    `CliCommand` has a new public field, `historical_sync_max_blocks_per_sec`, and
    `Client::sync_backward` takes the limit as a new parameter. Code that constructs `CliCommand`
    with a struct literal must set the field, and callers of `sync_backward` must pass the limit;
    `None` means no limit.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive-eth-rpc
  bump: major
